WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.908 --> 00:00:01.770
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:04.730
 People share stories with me
all the time that they've had

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:07.490
an ʻukulele sitting in their
closet for years, and years,

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:10.040
and years, and they've
never taken it out.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.960
So take that ʻukulele
out of the closet.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:12.880
Let's play some music.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.718
I started playing the ʻukulele
when I was 4 years old.

00:00:19.718 --> 00:00:22.010
I remember the first time I
picked it up and played it,

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:24.130
I was just overwhelmed
with this joy.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:26.690
Till this day, every time I
pick up my ʻukulele and play,

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:28.340
I mean, it's that same joy.

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:29.460
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:29.925 --> 00:00:31.550
There's something
about this instrument

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:34.460
that brings out this
childlike quality in everyone.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.490
And who doesn't want to feel
like a kid again, right?

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:40.580
And I promise you, if you have
a couple of fingers to spare

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.290
and you know how to
count, you will learn

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:43.457
how to play this instrument.

00:00:43.457 --> 00:00:44.570
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:47.120
Oh, that sounds so good.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:47.750
I love it.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.670
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:52.720
The beautiful thing about
the ʻukulele lies in its

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:53.530
simplicity.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.510
You have four strings, you
have two octaves to work with,

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:57.535
and that's it.

00:00:57.535 --> 00:01:00.160
Just take your finger and put it
in the bottom left-hand corner

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.150
like that, you know?

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:02.930
And that's your first chord.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.450
It's a C7 chord.

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:06.630
And then all you do,
is you jump up a string

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:08.040
and that's your F chord.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.360
And with that, you can
play hundreds of songs.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:14.208
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.110
I mean, literally,
hundreds of songs.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.520
Utilizing a tremolo
technique can be difficult,

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.550
but it can give
you that illusion

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.950
of the note sustaining
over a chord,

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.500
or for a duration of time.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.893
There's a magical
thing that happens

00:01:30.893 --> 00:01:32.310
when you're holding
an instrument,

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.560
and connecting with
it, and making a sound.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:35.920
It's like therapy for me.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:37.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:43.038
Music, I think, has the power
to truly shape who you are.

00:01:43.038 --> 00:01:45.080
And I hope that people
will discover through this

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:48.850
MasterClass that the ʻukulele is
not just a tool to make music,

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:51.970
but it's something that
can be life-changing.

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:58.950
Aloha, I'm Jake Shimabukuro,
and this is my MasterClass.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:02.600
[MUSIC PLAYING]

